shmringbench
============

This developer benchmark measures libshmring's sustained SPSC record and
stream throughput, then measures an idle record cycle using the same Unix
datagram signaling pattern as liblogcmp. It reports signal syscall and complete
idle-cycle p50/p95/p99 latency. Results are informational: qualification gates
should compare repeated runs on pinned, otherwise-idle target hardware rather
than impose a machine-independent pass/fail threshold.

Build and run from the source tree:

    make -C /usr/src/lib/libshmring/tests shmringbench
    /usr/obj/usr/src/$(uname -p).$(uname -p)/lib/libshmring/tests/shmringbench

Optional arguments select the record count and payload size:

    shmringbench 5000000 256

For live workloads, run these in separate terminals and stop with Ctrl-C:

    doas bsdinstruments watch shmring-traffic
    doas bsdinstruments watch component-ipc
    doas bsdinstruments watch bsdlog-performance

The profiles summarize operation and byte counts, backpressure/errors,
consumer-arm races, notification coalescing, channel depth, log batching,
persistence, drops, and flush latency. Enabling high-frequency DTrace probes
has measurable observer cost, so use shmringbench without tracing for baseline
numbers and trace a separate run for attribution.
